WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.367
♪♪

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.400
[ Laughter ]

00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:22.734
-George Washington didn't
actually cut down a cherry tree,

00:00:22.734 --> 00:00:25.801
so he [bleep] lied to us.

00:00:25.801 --> 00:00:29.601
What kind of psycho?

00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:30.667
So his dad sees it,

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.567
cut down cherry tree,
goes to him.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.334
Now we know, thanks to
the jackals, he never did it.

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:37.434
He says what happened here?

00:00:37.434 --> 00:00:39.901
And George Washington said,
I cannot tell a lie.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:41.601
I cut it down.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:43.033
To what end?

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:44.968
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:46.667
You know, we talk a lot about
how Donald Trump...

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:48.667
I think they've all been
[bleep] nuts.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:57.367
The Benjamin Franklin
key story as well.

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.734
Someone said
the Benjamin Franklin key story

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:01.968
is 1,000% fake.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.734
And all I'd say to that is,

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.300
Oh, something can't be
1,000% fake.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.334
Did I do that, right?

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:15.534
I think I did that right.

00:01:15.534 --> 00:01:18.300
I will say, very empowering.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.901
Get why you guys do it.

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:22.100
It's pronounced
zoo-aa--luh-juhst,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.467
not zoo-logist.

00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.701
And just a question
for the zoologists.

00:01:26.701 --> 00:01:30.667
When they told you that was
going to be the pronunciation,

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.067
none of you wanted
to step in?

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:35.234
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.234 --> 00:01:37.200
Now, why didn't you wanted
to say, "Alright,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.367
but we're going to be
correcting people forever?"

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.334
Just give the people
what they want.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.167
Make it zoo-logist.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.100
Someone said,
oh, the problem is,

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.300
in order for it to be zoologist,

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.901
you'd need three O's in a row,
two for the zoo,

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:55.200
and then one for the ologist.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.300
Great.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.834
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.267
Be the only -- be
an answer on Jeopardy!

00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:04.734
Be the only word
with three O's in a row.

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:05.834
Guys.

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:08.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:10.734
Fingerprints
are taken digitally now,

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:12.901
so if Donald Trump
has black ink on his fingers,

00:02:12.901 --> 00:02:16.400
that means his Sharpie exploded.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.567
And if it's blue ink,
he robbed a bank.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:23.067
We said because
he got fingerprinted,

00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:27.868
it was going to open up the cold
case file in New York City.

00:02:27.868 --> 00:02:31.801
And I said we got -- there's
a break in the case for the

00:02:31.801 --> 00:02:35.367
Westchester Strangler.

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:40.133
Bunch of people said
they didn't think Donald Trump

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:43.067
would be capable
of strangulation.

00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:44.968
And they made a lot
of little hand jokes.

00:02:44.968 --> 00:02:48.067
And that ain't us, guys.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.834
We don't mess with that
2015 Donald Trump comedy.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:51.901
We've all been through a lot.

00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:54.501
We've grown up.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:57.100
Been through a pandemic.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.300
We left little hand jokes
on the other side.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.234
I think, though,
he couldn't be the West Side --

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:04.667
Westchester, excuse me,
Strangler,

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:07.934
just because of a lack
of sticktoitiveness.

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:11.467
You know, based on every movie,
you've got to really want it.

00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:13.067
And -- maybe a choker.

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.334
I think he'd be, you know,
I think he'd be like

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:15.734
halfway through the
strangulation

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:17.501
and he'd be like,
"Alright, I think you get it."

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:21.634
Off you go.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.100
I said he -- after he got
arraigned,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.434
he took the FDR Highway.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:27.234
A lot of you told me
it's the FDR Drive.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:31.534
Do you guys not know
about the FDR Highway?

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:35.567
It's right next to me.
It goes so much faster.

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:37.534
You're still taking the drive?
Alright.

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:40.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:43.067
Corrected myself,
it's not a slice of pizza.

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:45.100
Last week, I clarified
it's not a slice of pizza.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.000
It's a sector of a pizza.

00:03:51.734 --> 00:03:54.701
Not a triangle, it's a sector.

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.334
And someone said
it's actually a sector

00:03:56.334 --> 00:03:58.567
of the radian of the pizza.

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:00.634
You want to use the
correct math terms, and I do

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:04.167
and I can't wait to tell Tony
down at the slice shop.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:06.968
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:09.901
All these signs
got to get changed, Tony.

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:11.834
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.901 --> 00:04:15.167
I said Medicis.

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:18.200
It is not pronounced that way.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.300
I think the best way
to move forward

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.167
is I just stopped
talking about him.

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:27.534
I don't have a lot
to add about him.

00:04:27.534 --> 00:04:29.300
A bunch of you tried to teach me
the right way to say it,

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.968
and I don't think
it's going to happen.

00:04:33.968 --> 00:04:36.601
Oh, we talked about the straw
that broke the camel's back.

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:37.968
And then as Lindsey Graham,
I said,

00:04:37.968 --> 00:04:39.534
Well, guess what?
I have one more straw.

00:04:39.534 --> 00:04:42.534
That's not the problem
when a straw breaks a camel's

00:04:42.534 --> 00:04:44.300
back that you ran out of straws.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.701
It's that you don't need
another straw,

00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:47.968
you need another
camel's back.

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:53.868
You can't get high off hemp.

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.334
Could of use that advice minutes

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:59.601
before completing
my first drug deal.

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:02.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.934 --> 00:05:07.334
That was an embarrassing night
back at the frat house,

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:10.267
trying to stuff
that thick sailor's rope

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.267
into the bong bowl.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:13.968
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.934 --> 00:05:21.968
Keith Richards, we talked about
Keith Richards smoking, vaping

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:24.000
and noticing it was
a flavored vape.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.601
Keith Richards would not say
cotton candy.

00:05:25.601 --> 00:05:28.100
He would say candy floss.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:31.567
Candy floss is why British
people have such bad teeth.

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:33.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.868
Chicken can eat shrimp.

00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:41.567
Shrimp can also eat chicken.

00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:42.901
It takes forever.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:45.300
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.834
If you're a waiter
at a restaurant

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:49.968
and you have a table of shrimp
and they order the chicken,

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:51.200
you're not going to --
you're not going to turn

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.467
that table over.

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:56.501
They're going to be there
the whole night.

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:57.501
They're like,
"The chicken is good.

00:05:57.501 --> 00:06:00.901
Can I -- can I suggest
the algae?

00:06:00.901 --> 00:06:03.000
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.434
I said -- we played a clip
and I said,

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:12.167
when we play this clip,
listen for the audible gasp.

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:14.033
And one of you said,
You don't need to say audible.

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:17.868
There's no such thing
as an inaudible gasp.

00:06:17.868 --> 00:06:19.467
Is that right?

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:25.067
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:26.667
Oh, we always talk about
how the rule is

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:29.267
don't look directly
at an eclipse.

00:06:29.267 --> 00:06:31.667
Very helpful jackal pointed out

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:35.033
you're just not supposed
to ever look at the sun.

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:38.434
It's just on eclipse day
people forget.

00:06:38.434 --> 00:06:40.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.334 --> 00:06:42.234
Which is true.

00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:44.901
It's not like a non eclipse day
I walk outside, I'm like,

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.367
there she is.

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:48.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.067 --> 00:06:54.467
This is a note from a viewer.

00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:57.234
It's the sort of stuff
I have to sort through

00:06:57.234 --> 00:07:00.567
in my weekly endeavor to put
Corrections together.

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:06.267
Verbatim what they wrote.

00:07:06.267 --> 00:07:10.400
"Your backdrop features
two rivets in the center beam,

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.100
stage right of your shoulder
and two rivets

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:16.801
stage right of that which serve
no structural purpose at all.

00:07:16.801 --> 00:07:18.601
There are no other rivets
holding any part

00:07:18.601 --> 00:07:20.501
of that backdrop together.

00:07:20.501 --> 00:07:22.334
How's the rest of it
staying together, Seth?

00:07:22.334 --> 00:07:23.601
How?"

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:27.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:29.234
Well, that was [bleep] riveting.

00:07:29.234 --> 00:07:31.734
[Laughter]

00:07:34.801 --> 00:07:36.400
Hey...

00:07:39.367 --> 00:07:41.267
we're posting a poll,
you know,

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.100
trying to figure out
which is the best

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.467
of our Emmy submissions.

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:45.801
We posted on Twitter,
and a lot of you said,

00:07:45.801 --> 00:07:47.534
You know what? Why are you doing
it on Twitter?

00:07:47.534 --> 00:07:49.100
A lot of people,
a lot of jackals

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.667
have stopped using Twitter,
and that's a very fair point.

00:07:52.667 --> 00:07:55.100
We're on YouTube, and we're
going to try to post a poll

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.400
on our YouTube page.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.434
Go to the community tab.

00:07:58.434 --> 00:08:00.167
The poll is going to be there.
We hope it works.

00:08:00.167 --> 00:08:02.300
And once again,
we're going to say

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:05.033
we still think
it's it's 66 or 69.

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:08.534
And, guys...

00:08:08.534 --> 00:08:10.934
don't just pick 69 because,
you know.

00:08:10.934 --> 00:08:13.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.667 --> 00:08:16.200
To quote a former first lady,
"Be best."

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.601
[ Laughter ]

00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:25.267
Guys, I don't even know...

00:08:25.267 --> 00:08:26.968
how we got on --
I don't even remember

00:08:26.968 --> 00:08:30.767
how we got on hearse drivers,

00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:32.934
but we did.

00:08:32.934 --> 00:08:35.868
And it opened
a whole can of worms.

00:08:35.868 --> 00:08:40.400
Incidentally, can of worms,
Hearse driver slang for coffins.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:46.567
We said coffins.

00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:49.467
Many said caskets
are more common now.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:54.267
Difference,
coffins taper at the bottom.

00:08:54.267 --> 00:08:57.133
They conform with the body,
the human body,

00:08:57.133 --> 00:08:59.868
and save you a little bit
of money on wood.

00:09:04.868 --> 00:09:08.634
That's just the tip of the
iceberg of the hearse community,

00:09:08.634 --> 00:09:10.033
hearse commentary this week.

00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:12.434
It was pointed out to me
that, you know, hearse drivers

00:09:12.434 --> 00:09:13.901
aren't actually in mourning.

00:09:13.901 --> 00:09:15.667
It's just a job to them.
I agreed.

00:09:15.667 --> 00:09:17.601
I said they punch in,
drop off the body

00:09:17.601 --> 00:09:18.901
at the graveyard and punch out.

00:09:18.901 --> 00:09:20.701
A bunch of you said
they should return the hearse

00:09:20.701 --> 00:09:22.834
before punching out.

00:09:22.834 --> 00:09:24.234
Obviously.

00:09:24.234 --> 00:09:27.300
I assumed -- I didn't think
a guy would just drop,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.834
like, punch out at the cemetery,

00:09:30.834 --> 00:09:32.234
then his boss calls him,
it's like,

00:09:32.234 --> 00:09:33.567
"Hey, Gary, where's the hearse?"
And he's like,

00:09:33.567 --> 00:09:35.634
"Oh, I left it at
the graveyard."

00:09:35.634 --> 00:09:37.033
He says, "Why did you leave it
at the graveyard?"

00:09:37.033 --> 00:09:38.234
He's like, Oh, you're going to
need something

00:09:38.234 --> 00:09:39.234
to get the body back."

00:09:39.234 --> 00:09:41.400
"We don't want the body back."

00:09:43.701 --> 00:09:47.000
I also said, you know,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.133
they -- it's very important
for hearse drivers

00:09:49.133 --> 00:09:52.601
not to get attached
to the cargo,

00:09:52.601 --> 00:09:55.367
so much so they refer
to it as freight.

00:09:55.367 --> 00:09:57.767
In order for it to be freight,
it has to be in bulk.

00:09:57.767 --> 00:10:00.534
But I'm guessing there's a --
there's been a hearse driver

00:10:00.534 --> 00:10:04.033
who's tried to double up,
save a trip.

00:10:07.234 --> 00:10:09.934
Also, they don't call him
hearses anymore.

00:10:09.934 --> 00:10:13.634
They call them coaches
or funeral coaches.

00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:18.200
Man, I remember my high school
funeral coach.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.267
[ Laughter ]

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.133
"You call that grieving?!

00:10:24.133 --> 00:10:26.501
[ Laughter ]

00:10:28.968 --> 00:10:32.033
You look like you're
at a [bleep] cotillion ball!

00:10:32.033 --> 00:10:34.601
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:39.734
Am I supposed to believe
you missed this grandmother

00:10:39.734 --> 00:10:43.367
or you [bleep] hate her guts?

00:10:43.367 --> 00:10:45.234
You call that a veil,
Patterson?

00:10:45.234 --> 00:10:48.167
[ Laughter ]

00:10:48.167 --> 00:10:52.434
It's got bigger holes
than a tennis net.

00:10:52.434 --> 00:10:54.067
I could see his whole face.

00:10:54.067 --> 00:10:56.467
Wipe that smirk off it.

00:10:56.467 --> 00:10:59.501
I'll knock you back down
to the JV funeral squad.

00:10:59.501 --> 00:11:02.868
[ Laughter ]

00:11:02.868 --> 00:11:06.167
You'll be flushing goldfish
back down toilets again."

00:11:06.167 --> 00:11:08.834
[ Laughter ]

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.701
-That's.

00:11:16.868 --> 00:11:19.934
Coach Mackenzie.

00:11:19.934 --> 00:11:21.200
Rest in peace.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.634
[ Laughter ]

00:11:27.501 --> 00:11:32.100
Played a little, um...

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.434
Stevie Nicks.

00:11:34.434 --> 00:11:35.801
It was, you guys,
some of you caught it.

00:11:35.801 --> 00:11:39.033
It was a sound alike,

00:11:39.033 --> 00:11:43.968
because when you use
an actual song,

00:11:43.968 --> 00:11:46.033
it can't stay up
on YouTube forever,

00:11:46.033 --> 00:11:47.267
which is the problem.

00:11:47.267 --> 00:11:49.434
Some of you pointed out
episode 40 is missing.

00:11:49.434 --> 00:11:53.133
It's because we we played
an Eminem song,

00:11:53.133 --> 00:11:54.534
and I have no regrets.

00:11:54.534 --> 00:11:58.167
But you can't keep it up
on YouTube.

00:11:58.167 --> 00:12:00.834
If you use
an actual known song,

00:12:00.834 --> 00:12:03.234
there are music
licensing issues.

00:12:03.234 --> 00:12:04.934
That's why, like,
if you go on YouTube,

00:12:04.934 --> 00:12:07.801
you can't watch, say,
Carpool Karaoke.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.434
[ Laughter ]

00:12:12.934 --> 00:12:15.801
Hey, so we played
"Edge of Seventeen,"

00:12:15.801 --> 00:12:17.868
the Stevie Nicks sound alike.

00:12:17.868 --> 00:12:20.033
And the first line of that song

00:12:20.033 --> 00:12:22.234
is "just like
the white-winged dove."

00:12:22.234 --> 00:12:24.701
And many of you admitted,
which I really appreciated,

00:12:24.701 --> 00:12:26.300
that you would always
thought the lyric

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:30.300
was just like
the one-winged dove,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.701
which is so dumb

00:12:33.701 --> 00:12:36.200
that you just accepted that.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:38.667
Like, a beautiful song
was about what?

00:12:38.667 --> 00:12:42.501
A one-winged dove with just,
I guess, what?

00:12:42.501 --> 00:12:44.868
Like, flying
in tight little circles.

00:12:44.868 --> 00:12:47.367
[ Laughter ]

00:12:47.367 --> 00:12:48.567
I don't think
a white-winged dove --

00:12:48.567 --> 00:12:49.767
a one-winged dove
would be going

00:12:49.767 --> 00:12:51.434
hoo, hoo, hoo!

00:12:51.434 --> 00:12:53.701
[ Laughter ]

00:12:53.701 --> 00:12:55.834
♪ Sounds like
a white-winged dog ♪

00:12:55.834 --> 00:12:57.901
Like, she's singing
my [bleep] wing!

00:12:57.901 --> 00:13:00.934
[ Laughter ]

00:13:00.934 --> 00:13:03.667
Oh, I intimated that pigeons
and doves are different.

00:13:03.667 --> 00:13:05.400
That they belong
to the same family of birds.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:08.267
Pigeons are --
they're the same family birds.

00:13:08.267 --> 00:13:10.968
Pigeons are known
as rock doves.

00:13:10.968 --> 00:13:12.701
A name they're given because
pigeons are dumb

00:13:12.701 --> 00:13:13.934
as [bleep] rocks.

00:13:13.934 --> 00:13:16.434
[ Laughter ]

00:13:16.434 --> 00:13:20.434
Oh. I said, a flock of doves.

00:13:20.434 --> 00:13:24.501
It's a bevy of doves.
A bevy of doves.

00:13:24.501 --> 00:13:27.133
It's a flight of pigeons.

00:13:27.133 --> 00:13:28.601
It's all very interesting.

00:13:28.601 --> 00:13:32.767
You like knowing how to refer
to birds as groups, Shoemaker.

00:13:32.767 --> 00:13:35.434
So a bevy of doves
sounds like this.

00:13:35.434 --> 00:13:39.300
[ Doves cooing ]

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:41.367
A flight of pigeons
sounds like this.

00:13:41.367 --> 00:13:45.033
[ Pigeons cooing ]

00:13:45.033 --> 00:13:47.467
And a flock of seagulls
sounds like this.

00:13:47.467 --> 00:13:53.767
-♪ And I ran,
I ran so far away ♪

00:13:53.767 --> 00:13:56.367
-So bad news. You only got
30 days to watch this one.

00:13:56.367 --> 00:13:59.133
[ Laughter ]

00:14:02.133 --> 00:14:03.467
